mits/ebf33f1795432459606bc815ae092f436ab9363a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/3878a8c7e0f859164291957a30cc5c0359776e92/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-04T06:38:09Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-04T06:38:09Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"message":"test(salesforge): drive a refused write through every verb, not just reads\n\nThe error table only ever exercised a GET, so nothing showed what a refused PUT\ndoes. That matters more than it sounds: if the HTTP builtin threw on a 4xx PUT\nrather than answering with one, the client''s pcall would catch the throw and\nreport code \"transport\" with no status, and a vendor saying \"no\" would arrive\nlooking like a network fault.\n\nThe table now runs each of 401, 403, 402, 429 and 500 through the two new\nsequence writes and through enrol as well as the mailbox read, asserting the\nstatus-derived code and the status itself, which is exactly what separates an\nanswered refusal from a caught throw. Simulating a throw on the non-GET branch\nturns the PUT rows red with \"transport\" != \"auth\" while the GET row stays green,\nso the new rows are carrying their own weight.\n\nNo behaviour change, so the 0.20.2 CHANGELOG section is unchanged.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Rd3ikB7kHqnRBnoW1mX8sh","tree":{"sha":"acd43b6c743eccd744150d5c6d03fe33cfe5f9dc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/acd43b6c743eccd744150d5c6d03fe33cfe5f9dc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/3878a8c7e0f859164291957a30cc5c0359776e92","verification":{"payload":"tree acd43b6c743eccd744150d5c6d03fe33cfe5f9dc\nparent f6c32f5b7d11212a744fc60a9b897999a4fa32f4\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1788503889 +0000\ncommitter Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1788503889 +0000\n\ntest(salesforge): drive a refused write through every verb, not just reads\n\nThe error table only ever exercised a GET, so nothing showed what a refused PUT\ndoes. That matters more than it sounds: if the HTTP builtin threw on a 4xx PUT\nrather than answering with one, the client''s pcall would catch the throw and\nreport code \"transport\" with no status, and a vendor saying \"no\" would arrive\nlooking like a network fault.\n\nThe table now runs each of 401, 403, 402, 429 and 500 through the two new\nsequence writes and through enrol as well as the mailbox read, asserting the\nstatus-derived code and the status itself, which is exactly what separates an\nanswered refusal from a caught throw. Simulating a throw on the non-GET branch\nturns the PUT rows red with \"transport\" != \"auth\" while the GET row stays green,\nso the new rows are carrying their own weight.\n\nNo behaviour change, so the 0.20.2 CHANGELOG section is unchanged.\n\nClaude-Session: h