/commits/d1f697902f9173da674501eaaaf639df29a4647b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-20T04:18:29Z","email":"smn7818@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-20T04:18:29Z","email":"smn7818@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"message":"chore: declare the neutron workspace environment\n\nrust:1 (full, not slim: the verifier needs git, and vendored openssl/lua\nneed perl, make and a C toolchain). Verification is discovery-inferred\nfrom Cargo.lock (cargo test --locked).","tree":{"sha":"5b0236eb2692620e94b25bedc955184fa98c8441","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/5b0236eb2692620e94b25bedc955184fa98c8441"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/d1f697902f9173da674501eaaaf639df29a4647b","verification":{"payload":"tree 5b0236eb2692620e94b25bedc955184fa98c8441\nparent de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b\nauthor Nayeem Syed <smn7818@gmail.com> 1787199509 +0000\ncommitter Nayeem Syed <smn7818@gmail.com> 1787199509 +0000\n\nchore: declare the neutron workspace environment\n\nrust:1 (full, not slim: the verifier needs git, and vendored openssl/lua\nneed perl, make and a C toolchain). Verification is discovery-inferred\nfrom Cargo.lock (cargo test --locked).\n","reason":"no_user","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niHUEABYKAB0WIQRV/FXDL+YfHsAYcfER+ZP89e7hTAUCaoaAFQAKCRAR+ZP89e7h\nTAGBAQDaJhO78Q1IGPuwYaaVV03YaS4Y5WaWnIJY7XOa5/ZPMgEAgGczrKMxrqL3\nolyvPXWNL+JOIS1uFT+AInbxX0lcGAs=\n=kxO2\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----","verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/185259703?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/wizardsupreme","id":185259703,"login":"wizardsupreme","node_id":"U_kgDOCwrWtw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/wizardsupreme","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/d1f697902f9173da674501eaaaf639df29a4647b","node_id":"C_kwDORLuRW9oAKGQxZjY5NzkwMmY5MTczZGE2NzQ1MDFlYWFhZjYzOWRmMjlhNDY0N2I","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b","sha":"de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b"}],"sha":"d1f697902f9173da674501eaaaf639df29a4647b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/d1f697902f9173da674501eaaaf639df29a4647b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/35b0202ec2f306febcbc09c5ab57e9192e5bd3b8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T11:40:11Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T11:40:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Sign-in that says what the product is (#274)\n\n* feat(dashboard): a sign-in page that says what the product is\n\nThe hosted sign-in was a centred card on a flat rectangle: a badge, two\nfields and a button. Every operator running assay as the front door of\ntheir own product got a page that said nothing about that product, and\nassay''s own orange read as their brand because there was no way to change\nit short of hosting a stylesheet.\n\nSign-in is now a two-area composition — the operator''s story on the left,\nthe credential panel on the right — assembled entirely from whitelabel\nconfig. No headline configured means no story element, and the page keeps\nthe centred card every existing deployment has today.\n\nThe story is operator copy, never data: a headline, one supporting line, a\ncapped list of illustrative Label:tone:Status rows, and a trust note.\nAssay supplies the layout and three tone colours and reads no account\nstate to fill any of it, so the panel carries an illustration label rather\nthan announcing itself as status. An unknown tone degrades to pending and\nan incomplete row is dropped — a typo in env must not take sign-in down.\nASSAY_WHITELABEL_ACCENT re-colours the page from one variable.\n\nForm work: fields gain real hover and focus treatment, the error region\nreserves its space so a failed attempt cannot shove the button down, and\na reveal control flips the input type and re-masks on failure. The\nrecovery link now sits after the input in the DOM and is placed onto the\nlabel row by grid, so a keyboard reaches the password field before the\nway around it. Google stays outlined and secondary.\n\nBelow the split the illustration stands down and the brand, headline and\ntrust note stay above the form. The credential POST, the return-to\nvalidation and the upstream list are untouched.\n\n* fix(dashboard): the sign-in restyle must not break the pages sharing it\n\nlogin.css is the stylesheet for all three auth pages, and recovery and\nlanding put a bare .login-card straight into .login with no sign-in panel\naround it. The two-column grid stranded that card in column one, so both\npages rendered off-centre. The split is a flex row now, which centres a\nlone child and still lays out story-beside-panel, and the card carries\nits own measure again for the pages that have no panel to supply one.\n\nThose pages also missed the accent token, so following \"Forgot password?\"\nleft the caller on a page wearing assay''s orange while sign-in was blue.\n\nFixes a live defect the redesign surfaced: recovery.js swaps its request\nand completion forms by toggling `hidden`, but an author `display` beats\nthe UA''s [hidden] rule and .password-login declares one — so the reset\npage has been showing both forms at once. Restored with an explicit\n[hidden] rule.\n\nThe sign-in badge is now a whitelabel token rather than a hardcoded\nletter glyph, so an operator with a real mark gets it rendered instead of\nan initial in a coloured square. The submit button says what it is doing\nand restores its label if the attempt fails.\n\n* chore(release): assay-dashboard 0.4.6, assay-engine 0.5.14\n\nCuts the sign-in redesign. The engine bump carries the new dashboard\nassets into the published image — they are compiled into the binary, so\nan operator cannot pick them up without a new engine.\n\n* fix(release): assay-dashboard is 0.5.0, not 0.4.6\n\ncargo-semver-checks: constructible_struct_adds_field. WhitelabelConfig\nis a pub struct with no #[non_exhaustive], so the six new fields break\nevery external struct literal — that requires a major bump, which in 0.x\nis 0.5.0. assay-engine''s dependency range moves with it.","tree":{"sha":"9ee2c798e1897c1d092f12e2e34b4259f5fa6816","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/9ee2c798e1897c1d092f12e2e34b4259f5fa6816"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/35b0202ec2f306febcbc09c5ab57e9192e5bd3b8","verification":{"payload":"tree 9ee2c798e1897c1d092f12e2e34b4259f5fa6816\nparent de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1787139611 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787139611 +0100\n\nSign-in that says what the product is (#274)\n\n* feat(dashboard): a sign-in page that says what the product is\n\nThe hosted sign-in was a centred card on a flat rectangle: a badge, two\nfields and a button. Every operator running assay as the front door of\ntheir own product got a page that said nothing about that product, and\nassay''s own orange read as their brand because there was no way to change\nit short of hosting a stylesheet.\n\nSign-in is now a two-area composition — the operator''s story on the left,\nthe credential panel on the right — assembled entirely from whitelabel\nconfig. No headline configured means no story element, and the page keeps\nthe centred card every existing deployment has today.\n\nThe story is operator copy, never data: a headline, one supporting line, a\ncapped list of illustrative Label:tone:Status rows, and a trust note.\nAssay supplies the layout and three tone colours and reads no account\nstate to fill any of it, so the panel carries an illustration label rather\nthan announcing itself as status. An unknown tone degrades to pending and\nan incomplete row is dropped — a typo in env must not take sign-in down.\nASSAY_WHITELABEL_ACCENT re-colours the page from one variable.\n\nForm work: fields gain real hover and focus treatment, the error region\nreserves its space so a failed attempt cannot shove the button down, and\na reveal control flips the input type and re-masks on failure. The\nrecovery link now sits after the input in the DOM and is placed onto the\nlabel row by grid, so a keyboard reaches the password field before the\nway around it. Google stays outlined and secondary.\n\nBelow the split the illustration stands down and the brand, headline and\ntrust note stay above the form. The credential POST, the return-to\nvalidation and the upstream list are untouched.\n\n* fix(dashboard): the sign-in restyle must not break the pages sharing it\n\nlogin.css is the stylesheet for all three auth pages, and recovery and\nlanding put a bare .login-card straight into .login with no sign-in panel\naround it. The two-column grid stranded that card in column one, so both\npages rendered off-centre. The split is a flex row now, which centres a\nlone child and still lays out story-beside-panel, and the card carries\nits own measure again for the pages that have no panel to supply one.\n\nThose pages also missed the accent token, so following \"Forgot password?\"\nleft the caller on a page wearing assay''s orange while sign-in was blue.\n\nFixes a live defect the redesign surfaced: recovery.js swaps its request\nand completion forms by toggling `hidden`, but an author `display` beats\nthe UA''s [hidden] rule and .password-login declares one — so the reset\npage has been showing both forms at once. Restored with an explicit\n[hidden] rule.\n\nThe sign-in badge is now a whitelabel token rather than a hardcoded\nletter glyph, so an operator with a real mark gets it rendered instead of\nan initial in a coloured square. The submit button says what it is doing\nand restores its label if the attempt fails.\n\n* chore(release): assay-dashboard 0.4.6, assay-engine 0.5.14\n\nCuts the sign-in redesign. The engine bump carries the new dashboard\nassets into the published image — they are compiled into the binary, so\nan operator cannot pick them up without a new engine.\n\n* fix(release): assay-dashboard is 0.5.0, not 0.4.6\n\ncargo-semver-checks: constructible_struct_adds_field. WhitelabelConfig\nis a pub struct with no #[non_exhaustive], so the six new fields break\nevery external struct literal — that requires a major bump, which in 0.x\nis 0.5.0. assay-engine''s dependency range moves with it.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqhZYbCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfBgQAFvhrEW/j1Z8h2UUXfh3uxSV\nDiF3wBklB4LnNe5N3PxGVI1n+OJhN1TqDI6dmHiwq4jfs1920HB0XjhI/mmqQfGu\no6u/Y6Kk6MXhQqFHX3dB7P3s9TBzKWcXnH+aM7QX3BkJOGOKJXnqFuDsq2+5Xaxh\nvTFfv5viFNZcEQom/83Sp6uHt9/0LjJJxoMk8KUxbrX/3piWcFdy2c6mKqw98E+P\naeVJc49dElTjLvQaIBeFpArANUL1q5qCtgoVAPTgGq4rMC13YppaUb16eoyvrtjF\nn46EBOGbAyYS1ZWR1z6k3BfnceFdvUBEFWFulWmB5ZapV5qUeWSBUSv2eDDujuyg\nTkjVMD7/8noRlSiAgJtHFR4jwLRmJI/KQf2m/snNqUZAByMetbVH5q25T+NpYG2y\nx1qjxA1/ZIRhd6JQEWMQaWW5JrRtFZuybdP4joEgG+47h+w1a8XOsnI1JASLkF95\nnIzfvkgKXsjJos3/H7pCZMUEzJPxdHZ+kGMJDOoAep22mLvEvhBaudNYiSIPDrv8\ndnr1VN9JdaLu8vcn08ykr3oq+508zYizMBW90rdzxw3p9V2dgaMHFoFVjNsTxtwz\nqX/3HsinW2CpJp4SOJzDsSgAXCRb0UVlNTuwPhdkt/jUIZARcqjGpYMiW+Z05tjp\n4fIdvlUz9vWT58JUS6KN\n=S9ID\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-19T11:40:11Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/35b0202ec2f306febcbc09c5ab57e9192e5bd3b8","node_id":"C_kwDORLuRW9oAKDM1YjAyMDJlYzJmMzA2ZmViY2JjMDljNWFiNTdlOTE5MmU1YmQzYjg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b","sha":"de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b"}],"sha":"35b0202ec2f306febcbc09c5ab57e9192e5bd3b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/35b0202ec2f306febcbc09c5ab57e9192e5bd3b8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-16T23:39:50Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-16T23:39:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(vault): Vault / OpenBao KV2 read facade for drop-in adoption (#271)\n\nAn estate that already holds its secrets in Vault or OpenBao can now point\nat assay-engine by changing a URL. ESO''s vault provider, ansible''s\ncommunity.hashi_vault, `vault kv get` and curl all speak one dialect —\nX-Vault-Token, /v1/{mount}/data/{path}, a fixed KV2 envelope — and none of\nthem could reach assay-vault before, so adopting it meant rewriting every\nExternalSecret and every inventory lookup.\n\nRead-only by construction: data reads (with ?version=N), metadata reads,\nLIST, and an unauthenticated sys/health. Writes, rotation, sealing and token\nissuance stay on the native /api/v1/vault/* surface; any other method on a\nfacade route answers 405 and no other /v1 route exists to find.\n\nA Vault token IS an assay token: X-Vault-Token is presented to the embedder''s\nexisting admin-bearer / trusted-JWT gate as the bearer it already checks, so\nthere is one enforcement point rather than two, and the gate closure stays\nmandatory in the type signature. Rejections speak Vault''s vocabulary rather\nthan assay''s.\n\nThe mount is a label, not a namespace — stripped before the lookup, leaving\nthe assay KV path verbatim; any other mount name is a 404 rather than a quiet\nread of a different path. A stored payload that parses as a JSON object is\nserved field-by-field, anything else as {\"value\": …}, so both an\nExternalSecret naming a property and a single-string secret work.\n\nOff by default in the engine ([vault.hashicorp_compat]) and gated at compile\ntime by vault-hashicorp-compat.\n\nCloses #270","tree":{"sha":"395b1a04b8ddb5f9014b3167262c9b50c580e170","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/395b1a04b8ddb5f9014b3167262c9b50c580e170"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b","verification":{"payload":"tree 395b1a04b8ddb5f9014b3167262c9b50c580e170\nparent 06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1786923590 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786923590 +0100\n\nfeat(vault): Vault / OpenBao KV2 read facade for drop-in adoption (#271)\n\nAn estate that already holds its secrets in Vault or OpenBao can now point\nat assay-engine by changing a URL. ESO''s vault provider, ansible''s\ncommunity.hashi_vault, `vault kv get` and curl all speak one dialect —\nX-Vault-Token, /v1/{mount}/data/{path}, a fixed KV2 envelope — and none of\nthem could reach assay-vault before, so adopting it meant rewriting every\nExternalSecret and every inventory lookup.\n\nRead-only by construction: data reads (with ?version=N), metadata reads,\nLIST, and an unauthenticated sys/health. Writes, rotation, sealing and token\nissuance stay on the native /api/v1/vault/* surface; any other method on a\nfacade route answers 405 and no other /v1 route exists to find.\n\nA Vault token IS an assay token: X-Vault-Token is presented to the embedder''s\nexisting admin-bearer / trusted-JWT gate as the bearer it already checks, so\nthere is one enforcement point rather than two, and the gate closure stays\nmandatory in the type signature. Rejections speak Vault''s vocabulary rather\nthan assay''s.\n\nThe mount is a label, not a namespace — stripped before the lookup, leaving\nthe assay KV path verbatim; any other mount name is a 404 rather than a quiet\nread of a different path. A stored payload that parses as a JSON object is\nserved field-by-field, anything else as {\"value\": …}, so both an\nExternalSecret naming a property and a single-string secret work.\n\nOff by default in the engine ([vault.hashicorp_compat]) and gated at compile\ntime by vault-hashicorp-compat.\n\nCloses #270","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqgkpGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA13sQAGTPxMg3kLtzBSR6jYdARkpW\n6QZAipQckoJa5NhD5R5DBN3+yMynfjNRDlvldXqEUURL1xVKckEout/SF6c7FJ96\nu1GoWpnUR8Ii+ioqQ4zQGN/sJF34Y7F9cbgeIKiDC68ryXQhqlmbnJgLD51b8EYb\nDte4smOk8xhtTEcCaai8dawM2H83miKLys63m79ss7maJnBBHbm2wCYJbgiSaBHS\nh4s2MH2IVpdT/WVP2x0ReBdyhl/rE2yUxbOUcqsGGfeB3McYk68pxPYmKFKtEbPJ\nEYUUxQybeTZNr1IquCRLoRJ6zF2r3m8YZrotrp1HheYBPGkUF43oUoxkWbhCq2tz\n1vPXqfNpLTAsmKBlE+H51hozI3oao+kJWBi4uSIIrtts689obdbfb6yCbedtQ77b\nJITorg8sHAXQCgQw2PbMAjWL0oVnAn27b0XuA7jcGnkCv67Rv1AFQoLf2TpaSyqW\nPELHkJxnVa6Q+PMdAjxT0o4ftMsWS0RWsbojYwMqu1NrJoWkjYmdnq7OFc/syhOG\nYaeQRdzm4hT+DoiaB0eBh9pm5biOc2nqv1lOFBRLc8sacYVC3214yvDBGIGyYg3D\n38SLQOkgWzmt/KQ/gKi1tFvQgzksAGCvpcrezVnD7V281NP3Dx/mJ1tXKxcsyoDh\nUJcas6WbEMahMlLz19me\n=ciy9\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-16T23:39:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b","node_id":"C_kwDORLuRW9oAKGRlNzJjMjY2MDVhZTkwZWVhMzQxNTM1YjkzNjBmYzVkYzYxNDRkMWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7","sha":"06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7"}],"sha":"de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/de72c26605ae90eea341535b9360fc5dc6144d1b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-15T12:50:39Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-15T12:50:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(authz): in-process authorization engine and assay.authz module (#267)\n\n* feat(authz): in-process authorization engine and assay.authz module\n\nScripts that gate an action on who is asking had two options, both bad:\nhand-rolled `if` ladders that drift between scripts, or a network hop to a\npolicy service a host-management script cannot depend on being up.\n\nThe new `assay-authz` crate decides in-process, with no I/O, no storage and no\nexpression language to sandbox. The policy is data and the vocabulary is closed\nby construction, which is what makes it safe to let an agent author one.\n\nSemantics, all of which fail toward less access: deny wins over any allow in the\nresolved scope chain; a condition that cannot be evaluated withdraws an allow\nbut leaves a deny standing; a grant''s bounds narrow the allow statements of the\npolicy it confers and never its denies; a malformed or undeclared scope entry\ndenies outright rather than quietly evaluating a different chain; and an action\nnames a base covering a closed, enumerable family of derived actions rather than\na wildcard.\n\nThe engine is decision-identical to the agentauthz reference library. All 149 of\nits language-neutral golden fixtures are vendored verbatim and run on every\nbuild, through both the pure evaluator and the composed engine. The four marked\n`storable: false` name a conditions shape a storage layer must refuse at rest;\nwith no storage layer here, the suite asserts validate refuses them and the\nevaluator still fails closed on them.\n\nLua reaches it as `require(\"assay.authz\")` over a Rust builtin registered as the\n`authz` global, matching how every other native surface is exposed.\n\n* fix(ci): register assay-authz in the moon project graph\n\nmoon''s projects map is an explicit list, not a glob, so `dependsOn:\nassay-authz` in crates/assay/moon.yml resolved to project_graph::unknown_id\nand every check job died before it built anything.\n\nThe conformance fixtures join the assay_rust path filter too: refreshing a\nvendored case changes what the suite asserts without touching a line of Rust,\nand CI has to notice that.\n\n* ci(authz): exclude assay-authz from semver-checks, wire it for release\n\ncargo-semver-checks errors rather than skipping when a crate has no\ncrates.io baseline, so the very first commit of a new crate fails the job.\n`publish = false` silences it, but assay-lua IS published and a path\ndependency on an unpublishable crate would break its next `cargo publish` —\nso the crate stays publishable and the check excludes it instead, until its\nfirst version is on the registry.\n\nrelease.yml gains the matching detect step and publish step. assay-authz has\nno assay-* dependencies, so it publishes alongside assay-domain in the first\nwave, ahead of everything that depends on it.\n\n* docs(authz): record the fixture provenance as 0.6.0\n\nThe reference library released 0.6.0 while this branch was open. Its\nconformance fixtures and its entire decision path — evaluate.ts,\nconditions.ts, and every model type the evaluator reads — are byte-identical\nto 0.5.0; the release adds an MCP server subpath and reparents the thrown\nerrors onto a named AuthzError base. Nothing this engine ports changed, so\nthe vendored copy is current rather than a version behind, and the table now\nsays so.\n\n* fix(authz): decide every context shape, and fail closed on unstorable bounds\n\nAn empty Lua table encodes as a JSON object, which matched no ContextValue\nvariant, so a check carrying `roles = {}` raised instead of deciding. A host\nwrapping the call in pcall would read that as a failure to deny: the reference\nsays an empty role list SATISFIES StringNotIn, so a deny gated on it must\nfire. Context values are now read by shape at the Lua boundary and every JSON\nshape maps to a variant, so a boolean, a number, a sparse table or a value the\nreference could only stringify all yield a decision. The verdicts were read\noff the reference''s compiled evaluator before being written down here.\n\nGrant bounds had no validation anywhere. The reference validates them at every\nwrite boundary, so a bound it would refuse cannot reach its evaluator; in\nprocess there is no such boundary, and a timezone-less date bound was being\nevaluated into an allow. The evaluator now treats a bound that validation\nwould refuse as unmatchable, and `validate_bounds` exposes the same gate to a\nhost that owns storage. Leap seconds join the refusals: chrono folds :60 into\nthe next minute where the reference cannot parse it at all.\n\nAlso: numbers render through JavaScript''s String(n) — exponential past 1e21\nand below 1e-6, and the Infinity/NaN spellings — rather than claiming parity\nin a comment while diverging; a grant or statement missing a required field\ndegrades to Malformed instead of aborting engine construction, so one bad row\nno longer takes the whole engine with it; has_explicit_timezone slices with\n`get` so a multi-byte tail cannot panic a public entry point; and grants\nauthored in snake_case keep their policy id.","tree":{"sha":"1cfa4692b553af73287b9aabb7757aa4ebfa7e62","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/1cfa4692b553af73287b9aabb7757aa4ebfa7e62"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7","verification":{"payload":"tree 1cfa4692b553af73287b9aabb7757aa4ebfa7e62\nparent 0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1786798239 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786798239 +0100\n\nfeat(authz): in-process authorization engine and assay.authz module (#267)\n\n* feat(authz): in-process authorization engine and assay.authz module\n\nScripts that gate an action on who is asking had two options, both bad:\nhand-rolled `if` ladders that drift between scripts, or a network hop to a\npolicy service a host-management script cannot depend on being up.\n\nThe new `assay-authz` crate decides in-process, with no I/O, no storage and no\nexpression language to sandbox. The policy is data and the vocabulary is closed\nby construction, which is what makes it safe to let an agent author one.\n\nSemantics, all of which fail toward less access: deny wins over any allow in the\nresolved scope chain; a condition that cannot be evaluated withdraws an allow\nbut leaves a deny standing; a grant''s bounds narrow the allow statements of the\npolicy it confers and never its denies; a malformed or undeclared scope entry\ndenies outright rather than quietly evaluating a different chain; and an action\nnames a base covering a closed, enumerable family of derived actions rather than\na wildcard.\n\nThe engine is decision-identical to the agentauthz reference library. All 149 of\nits language-neutral golden fixtures are vendored verbatim and run on every\nbuild, through both the pure evaluator and the composed engine. The four marked\n`storable: false` name a conditions shape a storage layer must refuse at rest;\nwith no storage layer here, the suite asserts validate refuses them and the\nevaluator still fails closed on them.\n\nLua reaches it as `require(\"assay.authz\")` over a Rust builtin registered as the\n`authz` global, matching how every other native surface is exposed.\n\n* fix(ci): register assay-authz in the moon project graph\n\nmoon''s projects map is an explicit list, not a glob, so `dependsOn:\nassay-authz` in crates/assay/moon.yml resolved to project_graph::unknown_id\nand every check job died before it built anything.\n\nThe conformance fixtures join the assay_rust path filter too: refreshing a\nvendored case changes what the suite asserts without touching a line of Rust,\nand CI has to notice that.\n\n* ci(authz): exclude assay-authz from semver-checks, wire it for release\n\ncargo-semver-checks errors rather than skipping when a crate has no\ncrates.io baseline, so the very first commit of a new crate fails the job.\n`publish = false` silences it, but assay-lua IS published and a path\ndependency on an unpublishable crate would break its next `cargo publish` —\nso the crate stays publishable and the check excludes it instead, until its\nfirst version is on the registry.\n\nrelease.yml gains the matching detect step and publish step. assay-authz has\nno assay-* dependencies, so it publishes alongside assay-domain in the first\nwave, ahead of everything that depends on it.\n\n* docs(authz): record the fixture provenance as 0.6.0\n\nThe reference library released 0.6.0 while this branch was open. Its\nconformance fixtures and its entire decision path — evaluate.ts,\nconditions.ts, and every model type the evaluator reads — are byte-identical\nto 0.5.0; the release adds an MCP server subpath and reparents the thrown\nerrors onto a named AuthzError base. Nothing this engine ports changed, so\nthe vendored copy is current rather than a version behind, and the table now\nsays so.\n\n* fix(authz): decide every context shape, and fail closed on unstorable bounds\n\nAn empty Lua table encodes as a JSON object, which matched no ContextValue\nvariant, so a check carrying `roles = {}` raised instead of deciding. A host\nwrapping the call in pcall would read that as a failure to deny: the reference\nsays an empty role list SATISFIES StringNotIn, so a deny gated on it must\nfire. Context values are now read by shape at the Lua boundary and every JSON\nshape maps to a variant, so a boolean, a number, a sparse table or a value the\nreference could only stringify all yield a decision. The verdicts were read\noff the reference''s compiled evaluator before being written down here.\n\nGrant bounds had no validation anywhere. The reference validates them at every\nwrite boundary, so a bound it would refuse cannot reach its evaluator; in\nprocess there is no such boundary, and a timezone-less date bound was being\nevaluated into an allow. The evaluator now treats a bound that validation\nwould refuse as unmatchable, and `validate_bounds` exposes the same gate to a\nhost that owns storage. Leap seconds join the refusals: chrono folds :60 into\nthe next minute where the reference cannot parse it at all.\n\nAlso: numbers render through JavaScript''s String(n) — exponential past 1e21\nand below 1e-6, and the Infinity/NaN spellings — rather than claiming parity\nin a comment while diverging; a grant or statement missing a required field\ndegrades to Malformed instead of aborting engine construction, so one bad row\nno longer takes the whole engine with it; has_explicit_timezone slices with\n`get` so a multi-byte tail cannot panic a public entry point; and grants\nauthored in snake_case keep their policy id.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqgGCfCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAoIIQACeGhg73kPFi9IaVf27dnF8M\ngizedIPTrkwNwPKeMZy+5iNAAGuJkGsaFonqg+MMvNSWj5eWKcnbRHrmE83SZESM\nXsj9iG5521jd5Ea0j5q0Qhnrr36Pjqn7eNURbY8HQqrcptkRTmfiNjufLnLmEmMI\nx51hqRUcR/pPy/9CUTf69jZo43w0lCRteYvMuPE/2S/XjID1goRwR5ferw7q6nmY\n203KvNz3kXsZAeTKO9QVgPezbCd0F3xOue9FbBvuj0NOyE49AYiPPzAtLvO0HOmb\nAz/wytCUZ2ocWCRTdgr49yBtvi1UPVYAUsHyEkbLLl27HHEeqUohuXy8Srbj7hVd\nN8nqldhGC4jT1nTCmt2mO1ivZmlQcTgnbOvpp5aL/PlDdkoqTa5Rn0JGcHuey8VV\nwZD13Xfkv9qVRhLtvLNCWGscntE/D62nT1fpf4ZGoUl290YaUMM23CMD00y+p330\nQHvuQeAOxiSjNe3JATPzDJ3NwOJGKyk8j1ZvssSX7hvHtt/3Lyz6EXYbJZWNhkpG\nfyPaN7qMH7cpAqZ5PyQt/ahlXVH/yZm+PVAUrtLSELeVLrnJ4d8B0yj6yGR+x5WB\nBP/ODIdNIGoUwzTMar33TVB2nozCpYYhXWHoM22l9/gWsz+kSTbzjnf82NmXrW6v\n38b66PeRIv6Beo13N5La\n=MzG5\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-15T12:50:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7","node_id":"C_kwDORLuRW9oAKDA2YmUxYmE1Yjg0Y2E4YzgxNGJhYTdkMjA2MjYxNmI2MDkzZTYzZDc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db","sha":"0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db"}],"sha":"06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/06be1ba5b84ca8c814baa7d2062616b6093e63d7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-14T18:11:12Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-14T18:11:12Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(metadata): @icon and @category module tags (#264)\n\n* feat(metadata): @icon and @category module tags\n\n`modules --json` described what every module does but nothing about how to\npresent it, so a catalogue UI had one shape for all 89 entries.\n\nModuleMetadata gains optional `icon` and `category`, parsed from `--- @icon`\nand `--- @category` header tags alongside the existing @keywords/@env tags,\nand both ride the `modules --json` projection. `icon` serialises as null\nrather than being omitted when no brand fits, so a consumer branches on one\nshape. The 18 Rust builtins carry a category too (core, and data for `db`).\n\nEvery embedded stdlib module is backfilled with a category and 32 of them\nwith a brand mark. Slugs were verified against the published simple-icons\n16.28.0 package rather than derived from the brand name: `argocd`, `fluxcd`\nand `sonarqube` are all wrong, and simple-icons carries no Amazon or AWS mark\nat all, so `assay.aws.*` and `assay.s3` deliberately have none.\n\nA discovery test walks every shipped module and fails on one that has no\ncategory or invents a thirteenth bucket, so the backfill cannot rot.\n\nCloses #263\n\n* fix(release): minor bump for the ModuleMetadata field addition\n\ncargo-semver-checks fails the patch bump with\n`constructible_struct_adds_field`: ModuleMetadata is a pub struct with all\npub fields and no #[non_exhaustive], so adding `icon` and `category` breaks\nany external struct literal. On a 0.x crate that needs the minor.\n\n* refactor(metadata): mark the module metadata types non_exhaustive\n\nAdding `icon` and `category` to `ModuleMetadata` forced this release to a minor:\ncargo-semver-checks classes a field added to an externally-constructible public\nstruct as breaking, and every future `@tag` would have cost the same again.\n\nModuleMetadata, QuickRef and DiscoveredModule are all parsed or discovered by\nassay and handed to a caller — nothing outside the crate constructs one — so\nthey are now `#[non_exhaustive]`. Struct-literal construction from another crate\nis closed, which is the condition the field-add lint keys on, so the next tag is\na patch. Same-crate literals are unaffected, so discovery and the tests build\nunchanged.\n\nBoth breaks are covered by the 0.19.0 minor already taken; the version is not\nbumped again. `modules --json` is untouched: the payload structs live in main.rs\nand are private, and all 89 modules still serialise their icon, category and\nquickrefs.","tree":{"sha":"d8160249aeaf250dde2f6745cd01430fee4e9936","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/d8160249aeaf250dde2f6745cd01430fee4e9936"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db","verification":{"payload":"tree d8160249aeaf250dde2f6745cd01430fee4e9936\nparent a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1786731072 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786731072 +0100\n\nfeat(metadata): @icon and @category module tags (#264)\n\n* feat(metadata): @icon and @category module tags\n\n`modules --json` described what every module does but nothing about how to\npresent it, so a catalogue UI had one shape for all 89 entries.\n\nModuleMetadata gains optional `icon` and `category`, parsed from `--- @icon`\nand `--- @category` header tags alongside the existing @keywords/@env tags,\nand both ride the `modules --json` projection. `icon` serialises as null\nrather than being omitted when no brand fits, so a consumer branches on one\nshape. The 18 Rust builtins carry a category too (core, and data for `db`).\n\nEvery embedded stdlib module is backfilled with a category and 32 of them\nwith a brand mark. Slugs were verified against the published simple-icons\n16.28.0 package rather than derived from the brand name: `argocd`, `fluxcd`\nand `sonarqube` are all wrong, and simple-icons carries no Amazon or AWS mark\nat all, so `assay.aws.*` and `assay.s3` deliberately have none.\n\nA discovery test walks every shipped module and fails on one that has no\ncategory or invents a thirteenth bucket, so the backfill cannot rot.\n\nCloses #263\n\n* fix(release): minor bump for the ModuleMetadata field addition\n\ncargo-semver-checks fails the patch bump with\n`constructible_struct_adds_field`: ModuleMetadata is a pub struct with all\npub fields and no #[non_exhaustive], so adding `icon` and `category` breaks\nany external struct literal. On a 0.x crate that needs the minor.\n\n* refactor(metadata): mark the module metadata types non_exhaustive\n\nAdding `icon` and `category` to `ModuleMetadata` forced this release to a minor:\ncargo-semver-checks classes a field added to an externally-constructible public\nstruct as breaking, and every future `@tag` would have cost the same again.\n\nModuleMetadata, QuickRef and DiscoveredModule are all parsed or discovered by\nassay and handed to a caller — nothing outside the crate constructs one — so\nthey are now `#[non_exhaustive]`. Struct-literal construction from another crate\nis closed, which is the condition the field-add lint keys on, so the next tag is\na patch. Same-crate literals are unaffected, so discovery and the tests build\nunchanged.\n\nBoth breaks are covered by the 0.19.0 minor already taken; the version is not\nbumped again. `modules --json` is untouched: the payload structs live in main.rs\nand are private, and all 89 modules still serialise their icon, category and\nquickrefs.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqf1pACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbPoQAIIgSCppdmrELtBpgACOP4ru\nVYzKIp3aOV/Wn0I6dRu9TXYG9n4miakhD1+TX+yU3xvmlQQMyq5bQksyd4e4hZ2b\nn8AwyYKQ+JM65zSPbBnQR8mC7nhaBVBE9OIbbVfc40rJZkk2y+itj+fbB0xGqwtR\nuG39nbnf6Ay/qelAm/dpTDANcZZ0f940unvIsD/98g7lCSeVBTQL2Rj2F0CGpKQi\nUsIJhV4msZrOC7NK6uLPvOPO6/1uDA/Y2qbWKPrbuZ7qWp78iUJsGlIT0cm8V6O5\nr3ay04CD+s3VEk3ePzIaYKWyLREhdekH3DoHuuZZU3VuNcCRoQuSYxxky+y/COp0\n9P+cHcl2ntu/uO6Sf0OwbrujH58Vr1nC2BhdKq2tlNXJCizcb94RWGBLlltSL+yp\nbsJYtlncgG1h8x49H2cOsTq4puZDPIViOap2+zVPCVRsnM61/XmdnmB+dyrK6XOM\nl+B9BYozQtFVmk5j8flHkP0u3xvL9uUYnhQSGiy1a9PupDRrSTQG9rfZX70glfs8\n2Df4/ndbO9JaLupGc+YJBzyaxBphl1WtTsUEgY9Y3Zg2cEErQi3pv5n33o7IOgZv\nCd04hdYgG4dMVKkDWz5E7YsTD0XmsO7zpg3Ylscg+YlmfP08Gn/JBaiK1YmuSQX8\nfPQrEIbekb4dk8ztqtFH\n=dFnW\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-14T18:11:13Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db","node_id":"C_kwDORLuRW9oAKDBhMzlhODM0ODg0ZDFmNTM3OGFkNjU1Mzk1NWM3MGNhOTQwODE0ZGI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5","sha":"a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5"}],"sha":"0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/0a39a834884d1f5378ad6553955c70ca940814db"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-14T01:50:53Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-14T01:50:53Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(cli): modules --json and an opt-out for the assay_context builtins (#262)\n\n* feat(cli): modules --json and an opt-out for the assay_context builtins\n\n`assay modules` only printed three fixed-width columns, so anything\nprogrammatic had to scrape the table and still lost the keywords,\nquickrefs, env vars and auto-extracted function names discovery had\nalready parsed. `--json` emits the full record per module under a\n`{ version, modules }` envelope, the version being the binary''s so a\nconsumer can cache the catalogue and invalidate it on upgrade. The\ntable is untouched without the flag, and the JSON path tolerates a\nclosed pipe instead of panicking on `| head`.\n\n`format_context` also appended the same ~1,300-character builtins\nreference to every response — roughly 325 tokens per call spent on\ntext an MCP client''s harness already carries. It now takes the choice\nas an argument: the `assay_context` MCP tool defaults it off and\naccepts `include_builtins: true`, while the CLI keeps the reference\nfor a human reader and gains `--no-builtins`.\n\n`test_format_builtins_always_present` is renamed rather than reworked;\nits assertions are unchanged, but the name asserted an invariant this\nchange deliberately removes.\n\nCloses #261\n\n* fix(context): keep format_context non-breaking on a patch bump\n\nAdding the include_builtins parameter changed format_context''s arity,\nwhich cargo-semver-checks correctly reads as a major break — on a 0.x\ncrate that means 0.19.0, and the release policy reserves a minor bump\nfor a human decision rather than a side effect of a feature.\n\nformat_context keeps its signature and its behaviour; the trimmed\nrendering is a second entry point, format_context_without_builtins,\nand both delegate to one private renderer so they cannot drift. The\nexisting context tests are untouched again.","tree":{"sha":"da7b0493f814afdb1b852a6a1bd2b2c6be09ca60","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/da7b0493f814afdb1b852a6a1bd2b2c6be09ca60"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5","verification":{"payload":"tree da7b0493f814afdb1b852a6a1bd2b2c6be09ca60\nparent 25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1786672253 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786672253 +0100\n\nfeat(cli): modules --json and an opt-out for the assay_context builtins (#262)\n\n* feat(cli): modules --json and an opt-out for the assay_context builtins\n\n`assay modules` only printed three fixed-width columns, so anything\nprogrammatic had to scrape the table and still lost the keywords,\nquickrefs, env vars and auto-extracted function names discovery had\nalready parsed. `--json` emits the full record per module under a\n`{ version, modules }` envelope, the version being the binary''s so a\nconsumer can cache the catalogue and invalidate it on upgrade. The\ntable is untouched without the flag, and the JSON path tolerates a\nclosed pipe instead of panicking on `| head`.\n\n`format_context` also appended the same ~1,300-character builtins\nreference to every response — roughly 325 tokens per call spent on\ntext an MCP client''s harness already carries. It now takes the choice\nas an argument: the `assay_context` MCP tool defaults it off and\naccepts `include_builtins: true`, while the CLI keeps the reference\nfor a human reader and gains `--no-builtins`.\n\n`test_format_builtins_always_present` is renamed rather than reworked;\nits assertions are unchanged, but the name asserted an invariant this\nchange deliberately removes.\n\nCloses #261\n\n* fix(context): keep format_context non-breaking on a patch bump\n\nAdding the include_builtins parameter changed format_context''s arity,\nwhich cargo-semver-checks correctly reads as a major break — on a 0.x\ncrate that means 0.19.0, and the release policy reserves a minor bump\nfor a human decision rather than a side effect of a feature.\n\nformat_context keeps its signature and its behaviour; the trimmed\nrendering is a second entry point, format_context_without_builtins,\nand both delegate to one private renderer so they cannot drift. The\nexisting context tests are untouched again.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfnR9CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAed4QAAARJcmIYcPPfpApaLExoOKH\nMM3WMAJWxVQYrRt6tY9dRP2HZViCb1fakd8l11AvjlIi0sbJqTclgrBkCL+l9Ezv\ntlNrMuQmHqZCnRZaqK+s4lviKM9JTx/Na6pTy6Dju7TET2YCzvQ590elKqWV+U5q\ntvbvYZTXXYCKfmGNS5h1fsHCMink4whSzKoXMLrdbkLw/2T8PCVE8RDRe5vahpBB\nwtvTcRmZ1IjLmwgLpp5E0FkdbjF5/P4lX+irHUH0PaaVa2HMs5shL5sfXBbkXBLl\n3MjB+opg3pHMSpwkYakEjEYAdil1NZtIxw3fUVx/kj/ak8/6QV3kC27P7fF6iBaw\nDvF5XT7Uxe0S6u4BYNmB/0gAOhhZMebTyAqhRgG6mY9UxCwNboDkS9HJh7AQ0jMb\nfQJfYMbw9ecYw4www/SU0G4TRJqbmG/6EIiJ9I1lIq3Wfl5X6L1Gr8nATpqZIlVz\n7gsqUqLREH3htO9Q/zxNFCVGfK1eX20lqoEg9fgFMtdiRY3bfYY4OjTGUpLcSGwU\ns9MxaMSKZYYceV5EDifXQqlYvssEZKaUhy2Q6cHx2Chv8j5c7BETxEHYCJhycuVv\nBfG42iLfZiun22Fe3xwQaWkm7gemEQEQUdXWWXB4TnxgyhKKqupQCLVGyo+umXN+\nRx6efzY6KJE5whONejEv\n=3LWM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-14T01:50:59Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5","node_id":"C_kwDORLuRW9oAKGEwOWE3MTQyNTY2NzZmMjQ1YTQwNGU3MDVlZmRiMTQxNWVlNmU2ZjU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2","sha":"25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2"}],"sha":"a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/a09a714256676f245a404e705efdb1415ee6e6f5"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-13T16:38:47Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-13T16:38:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(excalidash): ExcaliDash REST API module (#260)\n\n* feat(excalidash): ExcaliDash REST API module\n\nDrawings, collections, version-history snapshots, and user and link sharing\non a self-hosted ExcaliDash dashboard.\n\nThe credential decides the reach. An exd_ API key is exempt from CSRF but the\nserver''s scope gate admits it to only four route shapes; a session token reaches\neverything and pays a one-off /csrf-token handshake for writes. The client picks\nper route when it holds both, and refuses a history or sharing call made with\nonly an API key rather than relaying the bare 401 or 403 the server answers.\n\nScene writes and history restores carry the version they were read at, so a\nstale edit is refused instead of clobbering. A wrong api_path is caught too: a\ndashboard origin answers unknown paths with the SPA''s HTML and a 200, which\nwould otherwise read as an empty dashboard.\n\nstdlib_clickup.rs is reformatted only because the pre-commit fmt gate covers the\nwhole tree and it landed unformatted; no behaviour changes.\n\nCloses #259\n\n* docs(excalidash): quickrefs for resolve_users, collections, undo_last_change\n\nAlso corrects the history:restore quickref, which predated the guard version\nbecoming part of its signature.","tree":{"sha":"fc9b16893a8a3852566c3b2c6726dc081ccf663e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/fc9b16893a8a3852566c3b2c6726dc081ccf663e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2","verification":{"payload":"tree fc9b16893a8a3852566c3b2c6726dc081ccf663e\nparent 741f273d0766864c02e00c8b8d41adfd2d0a16e4\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1786639127 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786639127 +0100\n\nfeat(excalidash): ExcaliDash REST API module (#260)\n\n* feat(excalidash): ExcaliDash REST API module\n\nDrawings, collections, version-history snapshots, and user and link sharing\non a self-hosted ExcaliDash dashboard.\n\nThe credential decides the reach. An exd_ API key is exempt from CSRF but the\nserver''s scope gate admits it to only four route shapes; a session token reaches\neverything and pays a one-off /csrf-token handshake for writes. The client picks\nper route when it holds both, and refuses a history or sharing call made with\nonly an API key rather than relaying the bare 401 or 403 the server answers.\n\nScene writes and history restores carry the version they were read at, so a\nstale edit is refused instead of clobbering. A wrong api_path is caught too: a\ndashboard origin answers unknown paths with the SPA''s HTML and a 200, which\nwould otherwise read as an empty dashboard.\n\nstdlib_clickup.rs is reformatted only because the pre-commit fmt gate covers the\nwhole tree and it landed unformatted; no behaviour changes.\n\nCloses #259\n\n* docs(excalidash): quickrefs for resolve_users, collections, undo_last_change\n\nAlso corrects the history:restore quickref, which predated the guard version\nbecoming part of its signature.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqffMXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAoOMQADkYrHLlAc12lpBBTbFmtX0Z\nNLw/+dWi+S423aVthwis7SRJs2uaHHIjmLdytVhnyP3AKoYCU+BKBvwCKxRWa3ln\ntkuMyg55oIr/kdhMrCGfPTBAP2ICyTwGLVnsUVvuSCbkz3J3z8E03pPL3uxab0f3\n85m9J8CiD7xHeh6Wrfy0D8sgI8dKKGyPfR0scJtVNoT/vNpl0qkMRRD52zLE8VZN\nVTI8IjUoxOcewJ/bRgG7/apvROxLurFzlPoj70XzaWsiT6unK/C4wnTJUg5AMzqw\nHPElhdimc4cxNinQnk/vBTWmGjUGnJDgwSM+eiuh+XyjqAIgRixGcSatXoJzuGG5\n7shik6soY/KPcxjuxVQj96dQsTG78rhltcExDqrPGpXW6TJMHzEi3UMxWPpMzzTs\nJU/GGLpP2JLv0msDiIQCSYD0MgB3qfrVQ7PZzFFhe1IDdVvLkY1MhqK1mfVJvp3u\ntegxQUkXLKO3Opj5T+PuUwWshZcSBCYZ2bUlkpGk6hZpsw1lBZdtjC6NLsJc7MOV\nDydByflcl5BNpnCgoTyLto8yl+8mz7/oF3Rpy83cQh4m3wZkQH3dTv1qe7Da5XzF\nCqp1p2T9NBE1EcMcTZFToj/Zhh5owTvXYfYTvZ/bayqZWCSYzFx6FrnbdkcYgutE\n5sHhSLxWaFNuOFhahbl5\n=+xTi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-13T16:43:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2","node_id":"C_kwDORLuRW9oAKDI1ZmI2MzQ3YTE0MzMzOTZmOTFmYjA3Nzc4M2RhZjg1NWEwNmEyYTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon/assay/commit/741f273d0766864c02e00c8b8d41adfd2d0a16e4","sha":"741f273d0766864c02e00c8b8d41adfd2d0a16e4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/741f273d0766864c02e00c8b8d41adfd2d0a16e4"}],"sha":"25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/25fb6347a1433396f91fb077783daf855a06a2a2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92697?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/developerinlondon","id":92697,"login":"developerinlondon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjkyNjk3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/developerinlondon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/commits/741f273d0766864c02e00c8b8d41adfd2d0a16e4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-13T14:55:07Z","email":"developerinlondon@gmail.com","name":"Nayeem Syed"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-13T14:55:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(clickup): rich comment bodies and workspace member resolution (#258)\n\nCloses #257\n\nClickUp renders comments as Quill rich text. A string handed to\ncomments:create goes out on comment_text and is displayed verbatim, so\nmarkdown arrives with its asterisks and pipes intact, and a plain @Name\nin that string tags nobody and notifies nobody. The obvious call was the\nwrong one and nothing said so.\n\nclickup.rich() assembles the delta: inline runs for bold, italic, code\nand links, terminators for bullet, numbered and heading lines, and tag\nblocks for mentions. Line formatting rides the newline op rather than\nthe text before it, which is the part that is easy to get subtly wrong;\nthere is no table type, so tabular content flattens to labelled lines.\n\nclickup.resolve_member reads the person out of the workspace roster by\nusername or email, because the mention notifies on the numeric id and\nthe visible label is cosmetic — a guessed name reaches no one, silently.\n\ncomment_payload normalises builder, table and string alike, and copies a\ncaller''s table so merging request options cannot write through it.","tree":{"sha":"614ba92f9b0c992b7f5c0d748cd25da7095887e3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/trees/614ba92f9b0c992b7f5c0d748cd25da7095887e3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/developerinlondon/assay/git/commits/741f273d0766864c02e00c8b8d41adfd2d0a16e4","verification":{"payload":"tree 614ba92f9b0c992b7f5c0d748cd25da7095887e3\nparent a0dd286e1b0f8de557f17eb6b2f83be7587a3ac6\nauthor Nayeem Syed <developerinlondon@gmail.com> 1786632907 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786632907 +0100\n\nfeat(clickup): rich comment bodies and workspace member resolution (#258)\n\nCloses #257\n\nClickUp renders comments as Quill rich text. A string handed to\ncomments:create goes out on comment_text and is displayed verbatim, so\nmarkdown arrives with its asterisks and pipes intact, and a plain @Name\nin that string tags nobody and notifies nobody. The obvious call was the\nwrong one and nothing said so.\n\nclickup.rich() assembles the delta: inline runs for bold, italic, code\nand links, terminators for bullet, numbered and heading lines, and tag\nblocks for mentions. Line formatting rides the newline op rather than\nthe text before it, which is the part that is easy to get subtly wrong;\nthere is no table type, so tabular content flattens to labelled lines.\n\nclickup.resolve_member reads the person out of the workspace roster by\nusername or email, because the mention notifies on the numeric id and\nthe visible label is cosmetic — a guessed name reaches no one, silently.\n\ncomment_payload normalises builder, table and string alike, and copies a\ncaller''s table so merging request options cannot write through it.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfdrLCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5FsQACS3h9/Jltw/SBPmWBLu1D+R\nkrCdI53nolSIf2TbmPs1W7pRX1u///I/mJG8DFuYHz34OKYF6DyCdy9